12.1.1 Steps of Prim’s Algorithm

Initialization:

Start with a graph ( G = (V, E) ), where ( V ) is the set of vertices and ( E ) is the set of edges.
Choose an arbitrary vertex ( u ) from ( V ) to start the MST. Initialize a set ( T ) to keep track of vertices included in the MST, starting with ( { u } ).

Priority Queue:

Use a priority queue (or a min-heap) to keep track of the edges with the smallest weight. Initially, insert all edges that are connected to the starting vertex ( u ) into the priority queue.

Main Loop:

While the set ( T ) does not contain all the vertices in ( V ):

Extract the edge with the minimum weight from the priority queue.
Let this edge be ( (u, v) ), where ( u ) is in ( T ) and ( v ) is not.
Add vertex ( v ) to the set ( T ).
Add the edge ( (u, v) ) to the MST.
Insert all edges connected to vertex ( v ) into the priority queue, where the other vertex of the edge is not yet in ( T ).


Termination:

The algorithm terminates when all vertices are included in the set ( T ).


The result is a minimum spanning tree of the graph.
import java.util.*;

class Prim {
    // Edge class to store information about graph edges
    static class Edge implements Comparable&lt;Edge&gt; {
        int vertex;
        int weight;

        Edge(int vertex, int weight) {
            this.vertex = vertex;
            this.weight = weight;
        }

        public int compareTo(Edge other) {
            return this.weight - other.weight;
        }
    }

    // Function to run Prim's algorithm and find the MST
    public static void primMST(List&lt;List&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; graph) {
        int V = graph.size();
        boolean[] inMST = new boolean[V];
        PriorityQueue&lt;Edge&gt; pq = new PriorityQueue&lt;&gt;();
        int[] key = new int[V];
        int[] parent = new int[V];

        // Initialize keys and parent arrays
        Arrays.fill(key, Integer.MAX_VALUE);
        Arrays.fill(parent, -1);

        // Start with vertex 0
        key[0] = 0;
        pq.add(new Edge(0, 0));

        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            int u = pq.poll().vertex;
            inMST[u] = true;

            // Loop through all adjacent vertices of u
            for (Edge edge : graph.get(u)) {
                int v = edge.vertex;
                int weight = edge.weight;

                // If v is not in MST and weight of (u, v) is smaller than the current key of v
                if (!inMST[v] && weight &lt; key[v]) {
                    key[v] = weight;
                    pq.add(new Edge(v, key[v]));
                    parent[v] = u;
                }
            }
        }

        // Print the edges in the MST
        for (int i = 1; i &lt; V; i++) {
            System.out.println(parent[i] + " - " + i);
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int V = 4;
        List&lt;List&lt;Edge&gt;&gt; graph = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; V; i++) {
            graph.add(new ArrayList&lt;&gt;());
        }

        // Add edges to the graph
        graph.get(0).add(new Edge(1, 2));
        graph.get(0).add(new Edge(2, 3));
        graph.get(0).add(new Edge(3, 1));
        graph.get(1).add(new Edge(0, 2));
        graph.get(1).add(new Edge(3, 4));
        graph.get(2).add(new Edge(0, 3));
        graph.get(2).add(new Edge(3, 5));
        graph.get(3).add(new Edge(0, 1));
        graph.get(3).add(new Edge(1, 4));
        graph.get(3).add(new Edge(2, 5));

        // Run Prim's algorithm
        primMST(graph);
    }
}

1584. Min Cost to Connect All Points

Union Find
Union find is a good approach for this problem as we are essentially trying to identify connected cells in the board, and union find is a good data structure and algorithm to achieve this.
A couple of notes when applying union find to solve this problem:

It’s straightforward to identify connected cells with union find, but the end goal of this problem is to identify those cells that should not be marked as ‘X’. To this end, we could use a dummy node and connect it with all the ‘O’ cells that sit on the board borders.
Since we are traversing the board from left to right, and top to bottom approach, we only need to check the cell to the right of the current cell and the cell right below of the current cell.

In the implemention of union find, I am separating the union find data structure from the algorithms operating on it. Specifically, the Node class records two key information: 1) parent references parent of a node and 2) rank is used for path compression. The UnionFind class operates on the nodes defined for all the cells and the dummy node.

Java
class Solution {

    public void solve(char[][] board) {
        // create nodes corresponding to the cells in the board
        int m = board.length, n = board[0].length;
        Node[][] nodes = new Node[m][n];
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; j++) {
                nodes[i][j] = new Node();
            }
        }

        // connect nodes using adjacency rules
        Node dummy = new Node(); // to denote nodes not surrounded
        UnionFind uf = new UnionFind();
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; j++) {
                if (board[i][j] == 'X') continue;

                if (i == 0 || i == m - 1 || j == 0 || j == n - 1) {
                    uf.union(dummy, nodes[i][j]);
                }

                if (j &lt; n - 1 && board[i][j + 1] == 'O') {
                    uf.union(nodes[i][j], nodes[i][j + 1]);
                }

                if (i &lt; m - 1 && board[i + 1][j] == 'O') {
                    uf.union(nodes[i][j], nodes[i + 1][j]);
                }
            }
        }

        // mark cells that are not surrounded by 'X' cells
        Node dummyParent = uf.find(dummy);
        for (int i = 0; i &lt; m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j &lt; n; j++) {
                if (board[i][j] == 'O' && uf.find(nodes[i][j]) != dummyParent) {
                    board[i][j] = 'X';
                }
            }
        }

    }

    public class Node {
        public Node parent;
        public int rank;

        public Node() {
            this.parent = this;
            this.rank = 0;
        }
    }

    public class UnionFind {
        public void union(Node x, Node y) {
            Node rx = find(x);
            Node ry = find(y);
            if (rx == ry) return;

            if (rx.rank &lt; ry.rank) {
                rx.parent = ry;
            } else if (rx.rank &gt; ry.rank) {
                ry.parent = rx;
            } else {
                rx.parent = ry;
                ry.rank++;
            }
        }

        public Node find(Node node) {
            while (node != node.parent) {
                node = node.parent;
            }
            return node;
        }
    }
}

